---
code: cmdsub-operand-top
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# pi-webhost provisioning (curated package set from the fleet inventory)
set -e

apt-get update
apt-get install -y "$(cat /etc/webhost/pkgset)"
systemctl enable nginx
-- replay --
$ dorc plan --book=book.sh
note[cmdsub-operand-top]: apt-get operand 3 is a command-substitution `$(…)` /
   arithmetic / operator-form expansion, so I cannot know its value until the
   command runs on the host — there is nothing to resolve and no read-only probe
   to check. I skip commands only when it can prove the command's effect is
   already in place, so this one is left to run on every apply:
   --> book.sh:6:20
   |
 6 | apt-get install -y "$(cat /etc/webhost/pkgset)"
   |                    \__________________________/
   = improvement: If you give the operand a value I can resolve statically (a
      literal, or a variable assigned from one), then I can probe it and skip
      the command once it has converged. If your value must stay dynamic, load
      an oracle that vouches for this command's convergence, and Dorc will guard
      the command instead of running it every time.
   = more: 'dorc explain cmdsub-operand-top' tells the full story
$ dorc plan --book=book.sh --format=jsonl
{"code":"cmdsub-operand-top","severity":"note"}
$ dorc explain cmdsub-operand-top
cmdsub-operand-top

Dorc plans by resolving, before anything runs, the values a command will
run with. A literal operand is its own value; a variable operand
resolves through the book's own assignments, the same way you resolve it
when you read the script. An operand produced by a command-substitution
- the `$(...)` form - has no value until the inner command actually runs
on the host, and at planning time it has not run and will not. There is
nothing to resolve, nothing to hand to an oracle's own argument
checking, and nothing a read-only probe could measure. The line keeps
its place in every plan, and this note is Dorc saying so out loud
rather than quietly.

   > in your last plan:
   > book.sh:6  apt-get install -y "$(cat /etc/webhost/pkgset)"
   >            operand 3 is the unresolvable one; this is the line
   >            that stayed, and this note is its reason.

This is a narrower problem than an undescribed command, and a cheaper
one to live with. It is worth holding the two apart:

   > $ dorc explain --terse wall
   > A wall is a command in your book that Dorc knows nothing about: no
   > fact measured above it can be trusted below it, so the commands
   > after a wall are re-checked or run instead of being removed from
   > the plan.

A wall is about an effect Dorc cannot bound; this note is about a value
Dorc cannot know. Here the command itself may be perfectly
well-described - apt-get has an oracle out of the box - but the
description cannot be applied, because nobody can say which packages
the line is about until the substitution runs. The two problems often
travel together: a line like this one both runs every time (this note)
and, if what it installs is unknowable, is harder for the lines below
it to reason past.

What you can do about it, wearing either hat:

As the book's author, the cheap fix is spelling the value so it
resolves: a literal list, or a variable assigned from one earlier in
the book. Ordinary shell habits are enough; Dorc follows plain
variable flow. If the value genuinely must come from the host at run
time - a fleet inventory file, like this one - that is a legitimate
design, and you are choosing to pay this line on every apply.

As an oracle author, you cannot make the value knowable, but you can
still buy the guard: a convergence check that answers for the dynamic
form lets Dorc place that check in front of the line, so on converged
days the expensive install is a cheap read instead.

see also: dorc explain wall; dorc explain plan-lifecycle;
dorc explain oracle
